This code uses the Debug.Assert method in csharp which allows you to verify conditions that must be true during the execution of a method. If the condition is false, an error is thrown, otherwise the program continues running.

The first argument of the Debug.Assert method is the condition that you want to ensure is true. If it is false, the Debug.Assert won't pass and throw an exception, if successful it will continue running.

The second argument of the Debug.Assert method gives the error message if the condition fails. In this case, the error message is set to "If the return value is non-negative, list counts of new and old array are equal."--------------------The requested output is a csharp code that outputs Debug.Assert statements for the conditions that can be derived from the input code based on the comments. Here is the output: 

```cs
Debug.Assert(New_Ret != -1);
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListCount == New_arrListCount);
```

Explanation: 

The first debug.Assert checks that the IndexOf method gives us a non-negative value (-1 is the default return value when the item is not found), if it didn't return a non-negative value, that means the item was not found or we have a problem with our ArrayList. This result could mean we have an item that is being added but the method IndexOf cannot find it.

The second debug.assert checks that the count of the array lists is the same before and after. This could be a sign that an array we're testing doesn’t really change after the operation. If our operation adds an item to the array, we would expect the Count to increase.--------------------```cs
using System.Diagnostics;

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        public void PUT_IndexOfContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);

            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int Old_x = x;
            int Old_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
            bool Old_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            int New_Ret = arrList.IndexOf(x);

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int New_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
            bool New_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= 0 && New_arrListCount == Old_arrListCount);

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_x", Old_x);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", New_arrListLastIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$New_ret", New_Ret);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_contains_x", Old_arrListContainsX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_contains_x", New_arrListContainsX);

        }
    }
}
```

This revised version of the code includes a Debug.Assert command inside the body of the method at the comment indicated. This command checks whether the condition within the parentheses is True. If the condition is false, the Debugger will break and report an error. This should be considered to be output code in contrast to comments starting with "@@@".--------------------Sure. Here is your output assert statement for the described comment:

